<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sixtus Affair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1917–1918</span> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sixtus Affair was the secret attempt by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-i">Karl I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> in 1917 to make a separate peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, using his brother-in-law Prince Sixtus of Bourbon-Parma, an officer in the Belgian army, as intermediary. When the French prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-clemenceau">Georges Clemenceau</a> published Karl's letters in April 1918, the scandal bound Austria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> still more tightly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sixtus-affair.jpg" alt="Sixtus Affair" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain News Service, publisher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_I_of_Austria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
